        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Rastafari</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1930 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, in the 1930s, among impoverished and socially disenfranchised Afro-Jamaican communities, after clergymen proclaimed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>'s 1930 crowning as Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> fulfilled biblical prophecy.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: there is no single founder and no leader followed by all Rastafari. Early preachers who proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> the returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> included Leonard Howell, Archibald Dunkley, Robert Hinds and Joseph Hibbert, and Howell was the early movement's leading figure.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, often read literally and as cryptographic, with the Book of Revelation seen as especially important; also Leonard Howell's The Promised Key (1935), Robert Athlyi Rogers' Holy Piby (1924), Fitz Balintine Pettersburg's Royal Parchment Scroll of Black Supremacy (1920s) and, for many Rastas, the 14th-century Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">An estimated 700,000 to 1,000,000 worldwide in 2012; 29,026 people identified as Rastas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and about 5,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in the 2001 census.</span></div>
