    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Rastas worship one God, Jah, whom they hold to be present within each individual; they accord key importance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, whom many see as the Second Coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and Jah incarnate and others as a human prophet.</li><li>Rastafari is Afrocentric: it teaches that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> lives in exile in &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>&quot;, meaning Western society, and many Rastas look to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> as &quot;Zion&quot;, although since the 1980s many understand the return in a metaphorical sense.</li><li>There is no central authority, no creed and no professional priesthood. Rastas call their practices &quot;livity&quot;, and the movement is divided into &quot;mansions&quot;, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyabingi">Nyabinghi</a>, the Bobo Ashanti and the Twelve Tribes of Israel are the most prominent.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Many Rastas speak of &quot;knowing&quot; Jah rather than &quot;believing&quot; in him, and they use &quot;I&quot; in place of &quot;me&quot;, &quot;I and I&quot; in place of &quot;we&quot; and &quot;downpression&quot; in place of &quot;oppression&quot;, because oppression bears down on people rather than lifting them up.</p></aside>
    </section>

