    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haile-selassie.jpg" alt="Haile Selassie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haile_Selassie_in_full_dress_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown; according to [1] and [2] an official portrait of which b/w copies were distributed by the Ethiopian government, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rastafari is an Abrahamic religion that developed among impoverished and socially disenfranchised Afro-Jamaican communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> during the 1930s, in reaction to the colonial culture that then dominated the island. It grew out of the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island in 1834, although racial prejudice remained prevalent. Scholars have called it a new religious movement, a social movement, an Afro-Caribbean religion and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> religion rooted in African, European and Indian practices, while many Rastas prefer to call it a "way of life", a "philosophy" or a "spirituality". Its name comes from "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Tafari Makonnen", the pre-regnal title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, and it is unknown why the early Rastas chose this form of his name.</p><p>Christian Revivalism, including the Great Revival of 1860–61, Ethiopianism and the Back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> ethos, both with 18th-century roots, and the activism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-garvey">Marcus Garvey</a> all contributed. After Haile Selassie was crowned Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1930, several Jamaican clergymen claimed that this was evidence that he was a black messiah prophesied in the Book of Revelation, the Book of Daniel and Psalms. Street preachers, among them Leonard Howell, Archibald Dunkley, Robert Hinds and Joseph Hibbert, began to say that he was the returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, first in Kingston and then across Jamaica, especially among poor communities hit by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. Peter B. Clarke stated that "to all intents and purposes this was the beginning" of Rastafari. Garvey, whom Rastas hold in great esteem and often regard as a prophet, knew of the religion but took a largely negative view of it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rastas call their worldview "Rastalogy", but no summary of it has been given the status of a catechism or creed, and no Rasta has the authority to declare which beliefs are orthodox. Rastas are monotheists who worship a single God, Jah, a shortened form of "Jehovah". They believe in his immanence, summed up in the aphorism "God is man and man is God". They regard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a black African and reject the common depiction of him as a white European. Rastas accord key importance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> from 1930 to 1974. Many see him as the Second Coming of Jesus and Jah incarnate, while others regard him as a messenger of God or a human prophet. Members of the Twelve Tribes of Israel hold that the Second Coming has yet to occur. In a 1967 interview Selassie, who described himself as a devout Christian, said that he was a man and mortal.</p><p>Rastafari is Afrocentric. Rastas identify with the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> and, for most of them, black Africans are God's chosen people. They teach that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> lives in exile in "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>", their term for Western society, and they see Zion as an ideal, applied to Ethiopia, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> more broadly, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> or to a state of mind. For its first three decades the religion stressed return to Africa; by its fourth decade many Rastas understood this metaphorically, and the phrase "liberation before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a>" came into use. Rastafari is also millenarian: many Rastas expect Babylon to be overthrown on a Day of Judgment and a new age of peace and justice to follow. Views on race vary, and black supremacy, common early in the movement, has waned since at least the 1970s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast.jpg" alt="Kebra Nagast" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_De_ontmoeting_tussen_Solomon_en_Sheba_TMnr_5956-6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Rastafari treats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> as a holy book central to its belief system. Rastas often read it literally and use it as a source book from which to form their own beliefs and practices. They commonly believe that the Bible was originally written on stone in the Ethiopian language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>, a belief that runs contrary to scholarly understandings of the Bible's history. They hold that its true meaning has been warped by mistranslation and by deliberate manipulation by those seeking to deny black Africans their history, and they regard it as cryptographic, with hidden meanings that intuition and meditation on the "book within" can reveal. The Book of Revelation is often seen as particularly important for understanding the world's present situation.</p><p>Because of what they regard as the Bible's corruption, Rastas also turn to other sources that they believe shed light on African history. These include Leonard Howell's 1935 work The Promised Key, Robert Athlyi Rogers' 1924 book Holy Piby and Fitz Balintine Pettersburg's 1920s work, the Royal Parchment Scroll of Black Supremacy. Many Rastas also treat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast</a>, a 14th-century Ethiopian text, as a source through which to interpret the Bible.</p><p>Music is the movement's chief means of communication. Rasta songs are sung to praise and commune with Jah, and many use the tunes of older Christian hymns. Rastas also have their own language, called "dread talk", "Iyaric" or "Rasta talk", developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in the 1940s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bob-marley.jpg" alt="Bob Marley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bob_Marley_circa_1976_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Not credited, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>During the 1950s Rastafari grew rapidly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and spread to other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In the 1960s and 1970s it gained respectability in Jamaica and visibility abroad through the popularity of Rastafari-inspired reggae musicians, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a>. In the mid-1970s Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley</a> used Rasta imagery to bolster his popularity, and Rasta symbols became increasingly mainstream in Jamaican society. Enthusiasm declined in the 1980s after the deaths of Selassie in 1975 and Marley in 1981, when Pentecostal and other Charismatic Christian groups attracted more young recruits in Jamaica. The movement has survived and has a presence in many parts of the world.</p><p>Jamaican migrants took Rastafari to the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in the 1960s and 1970s. It spread across the eastern Caribbean in the 1970s, and Rastas took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a>'s New Jewel Movement of 1979. Small communities exist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Caribbean Rastas first arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> in the 1960s, and a Rasta settlement was established in Shashamane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, on land made available through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>'s Ethiopian World Federation; it peaked at 2,000 people and declined to around 200. There were at least 12,000 Rastas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 2008. In the United Kingdom, Rastafari attracted converts among the second generation of British Caribbean people by the late 1960s. It has also appeared in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Israel. It has spread mainly in Anglophone regions, largely because reggae is produced in English.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rastafari has no single administrative structure and no single leader, and most Rastas avoid centralised, hierarchical structures because they do not want to replicate those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. It has no professional priesthood, since Rastas believe that no mediator is needed between the worshipper and Jah, but it does have "elders", an honorific for Rastas of good reputation. Rastas hold "groundings", meetings with drumming, chanting, hymns, reasoning and, often, the smoking of cannabis, which is regarded as a sacrament. The largest gatherings, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyabingi">Nyabinghi</a> Issemblies, usually last between three and seven days. Rastas follow "ital" dietary rules, many wear dreadlocks and most avoid alcohol and hard drugs. The movement has traditionally presented women as subordinate to men, a pattern that has changed since the 1970s with a growing "womanist" movement.</p><p>Its subdivisions are called "houses" or "mansions". The House of Nyabinghi, probably the largest, keeps closest to the movement's 1940s form. The Bobo Ashanti was founded by Emanuel Charles Edwards in 1958, and the Twelve Tribes of Israel by Vernon Carrington in 1968; it is regarded as the most liberal form and closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><p>For its first thirty years Rastafari was in conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>'s authorities. Police often arrested Rastas for cannabis possession, and in 1958 militants tried to capture Kingston and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-town">Spanish Town</a>. The Coral Gardens incident of 1963 led to several deaths, and in 1964 the government introduced tougher cannabis laws. In 1989 a British Industrial Tribunal concluded that Rastafarians could be considered an ethnic group under the Race Relations Act 1976.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rastafari's social influence has been more substantial than its numbers, particularly in fostering a racial, political and cultural consciousness among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> and Africans themselves. Internationally it has been most popular among the poor and marginalised youth, and by valorising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and blackness it offers young people in the diaspora a positive identity. Its music shaped ska, reggae and dub. Reggae drew on Rasta rhythms, chants, language and social critiques and gained wide international popularity in the mid-1970s; many Rastas grew critical of it, believing it had commercialised their religion, and most Rastas do not listen to reggae. Rasta terms have also entered wider Jamaican speech.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, Rastas are no longer only the poor: after 1965 the religion attracted many middle-class members, and by the 1980s some Rastas worked as lawyers and university professors. In the 1980s Barbara Makeda Blake Hannah became a Rasta senator in Jamaica's Parliament. From the 1990s Jamaican Rastas organised politically, and in 1995 the Rastafari Centralization Organization was established. Rastas have campaigned for the legalisation of cannabis: Jamaica decriminalised personal possession of up to two ounces in 2015, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> legalised its use in religious settings in 2019.</p><p>Rastas have also won legal battles over the right to wear dreadlocks, among them a 2020 ruling by the High Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a> that all public schools must allow them. Rastafari is legally recognised as a religion in various countries, and many Rastas travel to Jamaica as a pilgrimage to the fountain-head of their faith.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Rastafari is an Abrahamic religion that arose among Afro-Jamaicans in the 1930s after clergymen proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>'s crowning as Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> a fulfilment of prophecy. Rastas worship Jah, see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> as Zion and the West as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, and have no central authority. Reggae carried its themes worldwide, and an estimated 700,000 to 1 million Rastas live today.</em></p>

